        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nDANIEL R. ORTIZ, ESQUIRE, Charlottesville, Virginia; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nERIC J. FEIGIN, Deputy Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent in support of affirmance.\nMORGAN L. RATNER, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; Court-appointed amicus curiae in support of the judgment below."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Daniel R. Ortiz",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ORTIZ: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nThe Eighth Circuit ruled in this case that someone in prison for something the court later determines has never been a crime has no means to challenge his continued detention once his opportunity to file a 2255 motion runs out.\nHe must remain in prison despite having done nothing wrong. But, as this Court held in Davis, conviction and punishment for an act that the law does not make criminal inherently results in a complete miscarriage of justice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 21-857, Jones versus Hendrix."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Counsel, did I misunderstand your argument? I thought one of the areas of daylight was that you thought it would apply even when circuit precedent changed as opposed to just when Supreme Court precedent changed. Did I misunderstand that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Daniel R. Ortiz",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ORTIZ: No, for purposes of this case, Your Honor, we accept the Government's position. We believe that there's not much daylight between its position and ours on purely statutory claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Eric J. Feigin (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ERIC J. FEIGIN ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT IN SUPPORT OF AFFIRMANCE"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Eric J. Feigin (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEIGIN: Well, Your Honor, I think it could potentially cover some of the situations the amicus has identified, although some of them I think, frankly, wouldn't even fall into 2255 in the first place."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Suppose it did, Mr. Feigin."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Mr. -- Mr. Feigin --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEIGIN: Well, a couple points on that, Your Honor. Number one is that, you know, you may or may not agree with us on this extending to statutory maxima, but that's not squarely presented in this case. Number two, Mathis in particular is an old rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But -- but Mr. Feigin says and they included the savings clause to make sure that in that codification they didn't forget anything. And so, when you then enter into this new world and there's confusion about whether this thing is actually in there, why shouldn't we interpret it to, consistent with the savings clause, allow it to proceed?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ratner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0003-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because Congress acted against a backdrop of a saving clause that didn't look like that, right, they wouldn't have expected the saving clause to swoop in and provide a remedy here. It had never applied in those circumstances for almost half a century. And so to treat the saving clause as doing something new as a way of kind of wedging an actual innocence exception into a statute I --I think is not consistent with the framing there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-857",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "41482b7e5c30df05b47c79e887c513a48bc6f046c53403788cee8c8e2d305684",
      "evidence_id": "ev-41482b7e5c30-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 73,
        "start": 73
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Thomas: Yes, counsel, could you take a step back and before Davis and before AEDPA? I think we spent a lot of time spinning a little -- a lot of different parts. What was 2255 designed to address, what problems, and how did it work?"
    },
